<%# ONE CATEGORY THAT NEEDS A HUMAN, AND WHAT CAN BE DONE ABOUT IT (answers-first spec §5, UI spec
    §4.2). Locals: `presenter` (HomePresenter), `category`.

    Split out of `_trouble` because the strip's other four arms are one element each and this one is
    a row, its rules and a four-branch fix — inlined, the `case` that dispatches the five kinds
    would be unreadable and a sixth kind would be added below the wrong `end`.

    THE STATUS SPAN IS `shared/_holding_status`, THREADED OFF ONE ROW OBJECT. Both suffixes travel
    on `HomePresenter::Row` — through the presenter, never a bare `category.holding_calculator`,
    because a calculator or a clock built here would date itself against `Date.current` and disagree
    with the rest of the screen silently — and the partial threads them together, so this caller
    cannot pass one and drop the other. It did: for two of the two-ledger plan's tasks Home's two
    bands printed different suffixes for the same category inches apart.

    THE RULES UNDERNEATH ARE `_holding_row`'S AUTO-EXPAND, RE-HOUSED. That rule was "anything needing
    attention opens itself, so trouble is never hidden behind a chevron", and this strip IS that
    population — so there is no flag to set and no collapsed state to render. A red row owes the
    reader the rules that put it there. %>
<% row = presenter.row_for(category) %>
<% rules = presenter.dated_rules_for(category) %>
<% fix = presenter.fix_for(category) %>
<div class="px-6 py-3 border-b border-gray-100 last:border-b-0 <%= "bg-status-danger-light" if row.status.state == :overdrawn %>"
     data-problem-category="<%= category.name %>">
  <div class="flex justify-between items-baseline gap-4">
    <span class="font-medium text-gray-900"><%= category.name %></span>
    <%= render "shared/holding_status", row: row %>
  </div>

  <div class="mt-2 pl-4 space-y-1" data-role="holding-detail">
    <% rules.each do |budget, due_on| %>
      <div class="flex justify-between text-xs text-gray-600 gap-4">
        <span><%= pool_rule_label(budget) %></span>
        <span><%= number_to_currency(budget.amount) %> · <%= due_on.strftime("%b %-d") %></span>
      </div>
    <% end %>

    <%# No dated rule to show is a real state, not a corner: a savings goal may hold none at all, and
        a rate category goes :overdrawn on rules that never carry a date. An empty box under a red
        line would leave the loudest row on the screen as the only one explaining nothing.

        Two cases, and `rules.empty?` does NOT imply a rate rule exists: :overdrawn is guarded on the
        balance alone, so a category with NO rules at all reaches it and used to promise the user it
        "refills at its rate each period" while nothing refills it.

        `category.budgets` rather than a count query: HomePresenter#categories eager-loads them. %>
    <% if rules.empty? %>
      <p class="text-xs text-gray-600">
        <% if category.budgets.any? %>
          No dated rules — this category refills at its rate each period.
        <% else %>
          No rules on this category — nothing fills it.
        <% end %>
      </p>
    <% end %>
  </div>

  <%# THE FIX (UI spec §4.2), CARRIED WHOLE OFF THE ATTENTION BAND. Every problem gains a specific,
      clickable answer naming a source that can genuinely cover it — or, when nothing can, says so
      in words rather than offering a dead button.

      FOUR BRANCHES, AND EACH IS A DIFFERENT ANSWER RATHER THAN THE SAME ONE SAID FOUR WAYS. Three
      of them decline to offer a button, and they decline for reasons that send the reader to three
      different places, so collapsing them would be the same defect as a silent row:

      1. THE MONEY IS ALREADY THERE. An overdue bill fires on a DATE and a missing payment, not on
         the balance, so a category holding every penny of its premium is still red.
         HoldingStatus#funding_gap is zero, and the honest answer is that the bill needs paying
         rather than funding — the same sentence the distribution screen's alerts band prints about
         the same category, in the same voice.
      2. THE MONEY IS COMING. Same principle one step out: the next distribution funds this
         category's whole ask, so a move today would rob another to solve something that solves
         itself. Read off the proposal this presenter already holds — see
         HomePresenter#covered_by_waterfall?.
      3. NOTHING CAN COVER IT. Every other party was asked, AVAILABLE included; see
         HomeHelper#fix_gap_sentence.
      4. A SOURCE — the button, and its consequence.

      The consequence line is AllocationsHelper's, off ReallocationPresenter::Candidate — the same
      sentence, from the same Data object, that the screen this button opens will print half a
      second later. %>
  <% if !fix.needs_money? %>
    <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 mt-2">Its money is already there — this needs paying, not funding.</p>
  <% elsif fix.covered? %>
    <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 mt-2">Its money is coming — the next distribution funds this in full.</p>
  <% else %>
    <% fix_path = { to_category_id: category.id, amount: fix.amount_param } %>
    <div class="mt-2">
      <% if fix.source %>
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 mb-2">This has to come from money you already have.</p>
        <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-2">
          <%= link_to fix_button_label(fix),
                      new_allocation_path(**fix_path, from_category_id: fix.source.id),
                      class: "btn btn-primary" %>
          <%# No `from_category_id`: the reallocation screen takes any subset of the three, so this
              arrives with the destination and the amount filled in and every source still to choose
              from — including the ones this strip declined to propose. %>
          <%= link_to "Take from somewhere else…",
                      new_allocation_path(**fix_path),
                      class: "btn btn-secondary" %>
        </div>
        <p class="text-sm mt-2 <%= allocation_damage_class(fix.candidate) %>">
          <%= fix.source.name %> <%= allocation_damage_sentence(fix.candidate) %>
        </p>
      <% else %>
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-600"><%= fix_gap_sentence(fix) %></p>
      <% end %>
    </div>
  <% end %>
</div>
